1
00:00:00,093 --> 00:00:03,390
In the last video, one weight had one slope.

2
00:00:03,816 --> 00:00:09,388
But a real model has millions of weights, and its error depends on all of them at the same time.

3
00:00:09,813 --> 00:00:11,529
So we need a slope for each weight.

4
00:00:11,968 --> 00:00:16,529
Bundle all those slopes into one vector, and you get the gradient.

5
00:00:16,968 --> 00:00:20,704
It is the arrow that tells a model which way is uphill.

6
00:00:21,639 --> 00:00:26,759
In this video you will learn to read a function of two inputs as a map of heights.

7
00:00:27,184 --> 00:00:32,543
You will compute partial derivatives, build the gradient, and see why it points straight uphill.

8
00:00:32,969 --> 00:00:39,259
You need video five on the inner product, video six on the norm, and video seven on the derivative.

9
00:00:41,006 --> 00:00:47,083
So far, our functions had one input and one output, so we could draw them as a curve.

10
00:00:47,522 --> 00:00:52,269
Now take a function with two inputs, x and y, and one output.

11
00:00:52,748 --> 00:00:58,054
For example, f of x and y equals x squared plus y squared.

12
00:00:58,466 --> 00:01:04,490
At the point three, four, the output is nine plus sixteen, which is twenty five.

13
00:01:06,019 --> 00:01:08,785
Think of the output as a height above the ground.

14
00:01:09,197 --> 00:01:11,910
Each point of the flat x y plane gets a height.

15
00:01:12,336 --> 00:01:15,939
Together, the heights form a surface, like a landscape.

16
00:01:16,378 --> 00:01:17,110
This one is a bowl.

17
00:01:17,535 --> 00:01:21,910
Its lowest point is the origin, where the height is zero.

18
00:01:23,439 --> 00:01:25,740
A three dimensional picture is hard to read.

19
00:01:26,179 --> 00:01:28,705
Hikers use a better trick: a contour map.

20
00:01:29,131 --> 00:01:32,761
A contour line joins all the points that have the same height.

21
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:38,984
For our bowl, the points with height twenty five form a circle of radius five.

22
00:01:39,397 --> 00:01:41,724
Lower heights give smaller circles.

23
00:01:42,176 --> 00:01:47,242
Here we draw the heights five, ten, fifteen, twenty, and twenty five.

24
00:01:48,159 --> 00:01:49,436
Read the map like this.

25
00:01:49,875 --> 00:01:53,478
Where the contour lines are close together, the ground is steep.

26
00:01:53,917 --> 00:01:57,029
Where they are far apart, the ground is gentle.

27
00:01:57,441 --> 00:02:04,210
We will draw the slope of this landscape as arrows on the map, without any heavy three dimensional drawing.

28
00:02:05,952 --> 00:02:07,495
Here is the main question.

29
00:02:07,921 --> 00:02:10,580
At one point, how steep is the landscape?

30
00:02:11,019 --> 00:02:12,814
The answer depends on the direction you walk.

31
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:15,527
So start with the two simplest directions.

32
00:02:15,966 --> 00:02:19,077
Walk only in the x direction, and keep y frozen.

33
00:02:19,516 --> 00:02:22,708
Or walk only in the y direction, and keep x frozen.

34
00:02:23,639 --> 00:02:30,514
Take a new example: f of x and y equals x squared plus three y, at the point one, two.

35
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:31,831
Freeze y at two.

36
00:02:32,256 --> 00:02:37,376
Then f becomes x squared plus six, a function of x alone.

37
00:02:37,815 --> 00:02:39,756
Its derivative is two x.

38
00:02:40,168 --> 00:02:42,416
At x equals one, that is two.

39
00:02:42,841 --> 00:02:44,863
So the slope in the x direction is two.

40
00:02:45,806 --> 00:02:47,335
Now freeze x at one instead.

41
00:02:47,761 --> 00:02:52,867
Then f becomes one plus three y, a function of y alone.

42
00:02:53,306 --> 00:02:55,965
Its derivative is three, everywhere.

43
00:02:56,391 --> 00:02:58,492
So the slope in the y direction is three.

44
00:02:58,918 --> 00:03:04,529
Each of these slopes is called a partial derivative, because it uses only part of the inputs.

45
00:03:06,039 --> 00:03:08,114
We write a partial derivative with a curly d.

46
00:03:08,659 --> 00:03:14,084
Read it as partial f, partial x: the slope of f when only x moves.

47
00:03:14,523 --> 00:03:15,707
The rule is simple.

48
00:03:16,146 --> 00:03:22,396
Treat every other input as a constant number, and use the normal derivative rules from video seven.

49
00:03:24,093 --> 00:03:27,457
Now bundle the partial derivatives into one vector.

50
00:03:27,882 --> 00:03:29,664
This vector is called the gradient.

51
00:03:30,103 --> 00:03:33,228
We write it with an upside down triangle, called nabla.

52
00:03:33,707 --> 00:03:39,545
For x squared plus three y at the point one, two, the gradient is two, three.

53
00:03:39,984 --> 00:03:41,194
It is an arrow on the map.

54
00:03:42,706 --> 00:03:45,618
Let's do the bowl, x squared plus y squared.

55
00:03:46,044 --> 00:03:49,169
The partial with respect to x is two x.

56
00:03:49,621 --> 00:03:52,081
The partial with respect to y is two y.

57
00:03:52,506 --> 00:03:55,312
So the gradient is two x, two y.

58
00:03:55,738 --> 00:03:58,304
At the point three, four, it is six, eight.

59
00:03:58,730 --> 00:04:00,312
Its length is ten.

60
00:04:01,226 --> 00:04:03,992
Draw the gradient at many points of the map.

61
00:04:04,417 --> 00:04:07,875
Every arrow points away from the bottom of the bowl, straight up the hill.

62
00:04:08,287 --> 00:04:12,330
The arrows are long where the bowl is steep, far from the center.

63
00:04:12,768 --> 00:04:16,013
They are short near the bottom, where the bowl is almost flat.

64
00:04:17,539 --> 00:04:18,683
A small note on writing.

65
00:04:19,108 --> 00:04:25,305
Some books, including the Mathematics for Machine Learning book, write the gradient as a row of numbers.

66
00:04:25,731 --> 00:04:27,393
Here we draw it as an arrow.

67
00:04:27,818 --> 00:04:29,122
It is the same information.

68
00:04:29,547 --> 00:04:34,986
In machine learning code, the gradient usually has exactly the same shape as the weights.

69
00:04:36,706 --> 00:04:42,357
In video seven, we learned the key idea: close up, a function looks linear.

70
00:04:42,796 --> 00:04:47,344
The new value is about the old value, plus the slope times the step.

71
00:04:47,770 --> 00:04:51,147
With many inputs, the step h is a vector.

72
00:04:51,586 --> 00:04:56,107
And the slope times the step becomes the dot product of the gradient with h.

73
00:04:57,039 --> 00:05:04,539
So the formula is: f of x plus h is approximately f of x, plus the gradient dot h.

74
00:05:04,978 --> 00:05:11,308
The dot product multiplies each partial slope by the step in that direction, and adds the results.

75
00:05:11,733 --> 00:05:17,850
This is axiom C, local linearity, written with the inner product of video five.

76
00:05:19,373 --> 00:05:19,918
Let's check it.

77
00:05:20,410 --> 00:05:25,263
For x squared plus three y at one, two, the value is seven.

78
00:05:25,702 --> 00:05:29,412
Step by h equal to zero point one, zero point one.

79
00:05:29,851 --> 00:05:36,021
The gradient dot h is two times zero point one, plus three times zero point one.

80
00:05:36,447 --> 00:05:37,883
That is zero point five.

81
00:05:38,309 --> 00:05:40,210
So the guess is seven point five.

82
00:05:40,636 --> 00:05:42,910
The true value is seven point five one.

83
00:05:43,839 --> 00:05:47,031
A second check, on the bowl at three, four.

84
00:05:47,456 --> 00:05:50,674
Step by zero point one, minus zero point one.

85
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:57,190
The gradient dot h is six times zero point one, minus eight times zero point one.

86
00:05:57,616 --> 00:05:59,398
That is minus zero point two.

87
00:05:59,823 --> 00:06:01,791
So the guess is twenty four point eight.

88
00:06:02,230 --> 00:06:04,597
The true value is twenty four point eight two.

89
00:06:05,023 --> 00:06:05,993
Very close again.

90
00:06:07,739 --> 00:06:08,617
Now the big question.

91
00:06:09,029 --> 00:06:12,606
Of all directions, which one goes uphill fastest?

92
00:06:13,045 --> 00:06:16,050
Take a step of length one in some direction u.

93
00:06:16,489 --> 00:06:19,189
The change in height is about the gradient dot u.

94
00:06:19,641 --> 00:06:25,452
From video five, a dot product equals the two lengths times the cosine of the angle between them.

95
00:06:26,373 --> 00:06:32,476
The length of u is one, so the change is the length of the gradient, times the cosine of the angle.

96
00:06:32,888 --> 00:06:34,963
Watch the direction turn around the point.

97
00:06:35,402 --> 00:06:43,008
When u points along the gradient, the angle is zero, the cosine is one, and the change is largest: ten.

98
00:06:43,447 --> 00:06:46,652
So the gradient points in the steepest uphill direction.

99
00:06:47,593 --> 00:06:53,749
At an angle of ninety degrees, the cosine is zero, and the height does not change.

100
00:06:54,162 --> 00:06:56,848
That direction runs along the contour line.

101
00:06:57,287 --> 00:07:01,316
So the gradient is always perpendicular to the contour lines.

102
00:07:01,742 --> 00:07:05,957
And at one hundred eighty degrees, the change is minus ten.

103
00:07:06,396 --> 00:07:09,228
Minus the gradient points steepest downhill.

104
00:07:10,139 --> 00:07:15,113
That last fact is the seed of gradient descent, the main training method.

105
00:07:15,538 --> 00:07:20,950
Take a small step against the gradient, again and again, and you walk down the hill.

106
00:07:21,376 --> 00:07:26,642
Gradient descent is a molecule built from these atoms, so here we only preview it.

107
00:07:28,359 --> 00:07:35,580
In machine learning, the landscape is the loss: the error score of the model, which we study in video fifteen.

108
00:07:36,019 --> 00:07:37,708
Its inputs are all the weights.

109
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:42,827
The small GPT two model has about one hundred twenty four million weights.

110
00:07:43,266 --> 00:07:49,662
So its gradient has one hundred twenty four million partial derivatives, one for each weight.

111
00:07:51,193 --> 00:07:55,248
Each partial derivative answers the question from the start of this video.

112
00:07:55,687 --> 00:07:59,038
If I nudge only this weight, how fast does the loss change?

113
00:07:59,490 --> 00:08:02,868
The gradient collects all the answers in one vector.

114
00:08:03,294 --> 00:08:10,474
Libraries like PyTorch compute it for us, and store a gradient with the same shape as each weight matrix.

115
00:08:12,019 --> 00:08:14,080
Here is one toy step on our bowl.

116
00:08:14,493 --> 00:08:18,828
Start at three, four, where the height is twenty five.

117
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:21,168
The gradient is six, eight.

118
00:08:21,594 --> 00:08:24,931
Step against it, with a step size of zero point one.

119
00:08:25,370 --> 00:08:28,336
The new point is two point four, three point two.

120
00:08:28,774 --> 00:08:31,607
The height drops from twenty five to sixteen.

121
00:08:32,032 --> 00:08:34,054
One step, and we are much lower.

122
00:08:35,759 --> 00:08:36,637
Now it is your turn.

123
00:08:37,062 --> 00:08:38,884
Pause the video and try this.

124
00:08:39,296 --> 00:08:43,179
Take f of x and y equals three x plus y squared.

125
00:08:43,605 --> 00:08:48,007
What are the two partial derivatives, and what is the gradient at the point two, one?

126
00:08:48,432 --> 00:08:50,733
Remember: freeze the other input.

127
00:08:51,686 --> 00:08:52,617
Here are the answers.

128
00:08:53,082 --> 00:08:57,550
Freeze y, and three x plus a constant has slope three.

129
00:08:58,029 --> 00:09:00,954
Freeze x, and y squared has slope two y.

130
00:09:01,367 --> 00:09:04,266
At the point two, one, two y is two.

131
00:09:04,691 --> 00:09:07,125
So the gradient is three, two.

132
00:09:08,652 --> 00:09:13,692
One more question, on the bowl x squared plus y squared at the point zero, five.

133
00:09:14,105 --> 00:09:15,155
What is the gradient?

134
00:09:15,581 --> 00:09:17,868
In which direction is the steepest way up?

135
00:09:18,293 --> 00:09:21,512
And in which direction does the height not change at all?

136
00:09:21,937 --> 00:09:22,801
Pause and think.

137
00:09:23,706 --> 00:09:28,201
The gradient is two x, two y, which is zero, ten.

138
00:09:28,639 --> 00:09:32,682
So the steepest way up is straight in the y direction, with slope ten.

139
00:09:33,134 --> 00:09:38,613
The height does not change if you walk in the x direction, along the circle of radius five.

140
00:09:39,052 --> 00:09:41,990
That direction is perpendicular to the gradient.

141
00:09:43,739 --> 00:09:44,909
Let's check this in Python.

142
00:09:45,335 --> 00:09:46,784
We write a numeric gradient.

143
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:55,694
For each input, we nudge only that input up and down by a tiny h, freeze the others, and divide the change by two h.

144
00:09:56,120 --> 00:10:02,064
Then we compare with the exact gradients, test the linear guess, and take one step downhill.

145
00:10:03,573 --> 00:10:07,429
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

146
00:10:07,854 --> 00:10:14,397
The numeric gradients are two, three and six, eight, equal to the exact ones.

147
00:10:14,836 --> 00:10:18,214
The linear guesses are very close to the true values.

148
00:10:18,652 --> 00:10:23,905
The slope along the gradient is ten, and at ninety degrees it is zero.

149
00:10:24,304 --> 00:10:27,509
One step takes the height from twenty five to sixteen.

150
00:10:29,239 --> 00:10:29,944
In summary.

151
00:10:30,370 --> 00:10:35,503
A function of two inputs is a landscape, and a contour map shows it flat.

152
00:10:35,928 --> 00:10:41,566
A partial derivative is the slope when only one input moves and the others are frozen.

153
00:10:41,992 --> 00:10:46,074
The gradient bundles all the partial derivatives into one vector.

154
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:52,417
Close up, f of x plus h is about f of x, plus the gradient dot h.

155
00:10:53,373 --> 00:10:58,958
The gradient points steepest uphill, and it is perpendicular to the contour lines.

156
00:10:59,396 --> 00:11:01,511
Minus the gradient points downhill.

157
00:11:01,936 --> 00:11:06,617
In machine learning, the gradient of the loss has one entry per weight.

158
00:11:07,056 --> 00:11:11,604
Next, in video nine: the chain rule, where slopes multiply.

159
00:11:12,029 --> 00:11:12,561
See you there.

160
00:11:14,506 --> 00:11:16,328
This video was made by David R.

161
00:11:16,767 --> 00:11:19,998
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

162
00:11:20,556 --> 00:11:21,274
Thanks for watching.
